  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nMuch colder air was arriving over the area early this morning with \nstrong H85 caa underway. Afternoon highs may struggle to climb out \nof the lower 50s in some areas. The highest wind speeds are expected \nthrough the morning hours while gradually subsiding as we move \nthrough the day as the sfc pressure gradient gradually diminishes. A \nridge of high pressure at the sfc is progged to settle over the area \ntonight bringing light winds under mostly clear conditions. This \nwill result in robust radiational cooling leading to areas of frost \ntonight into Saturday morning with the first freeze of the season \nfor parts of central/south central KS. The freeze watch was expanded \nsouthward to include most of central and parts of south central KS \nwhere lows in the upper 20s/around 30 are anticipated. Southerly \nwinds will return to central KS on Saturday allowing temperatures to \nquickly moderate with highs returning to the lower 60s while another \nday of upper 50s is anticipated across much of eastern Kansas. \nA stable and tranquil weather regime is anticipated through \nearly next week as the upper level pattern transitions to a SW \nmid/upper flow regime early in the week with breezy southerly \nwinds lingering. Seasonable temperatures will return on Monday \nwith above average temperatures persisting through the end of \nthe work week with highs around 80. Some low pops were \nmaintained late in the week as some subtle shortwave troughs \nridge over the mid/upper ridge over the southern CONUS, but \nconfidence in widespread wetting precipitation remains fairly \nlow at this time.",
